Z||000002||FieroHarry||11-20-2001||09:58 PM||||Okay I'll bite.<P>Just how the heck would you do "water to air"?<P>Seems to me using the cars cooling system would be silly.<BR>Inventing and installing a another water born system in a Fiero would add at least another 100lbs and would be hard to accomplish with the limited space to begin with....<BR>Maybe im reading something into what your saying, could you explain it better for me?<BR>Im kinna dumb somtins...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000003||filthyscarecrow||11-21-2001||12:36 AM||||you change the air paths, the intake charge goes throught the former frontal area, adn teh water goes through the tanks that the air used to. use a seperate water system for the whole thing. yeah, it's heavier, but it's also more efficient than trying to put an air2air in the back of a car with no airflow. it would weigh about 30-35 lbs, including water (al depends on the size of the header tank. i know a guy that made one that works great. same concept...||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTMike||11-21-2001||06:44 AM||||you could cut a hole bigger hole where the original air intake comes from, and put the air to air there with some fancy large side scoop. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Predator||11-21-2001||08:02 AM||||Actually at some point I am planning on installing my intercooler where the drivers side vent is possibly with a lazy -c scoop over that, and a fan to blow air over the intercooler. Please don't start flaming me about the scoop. I know the Fiero is designed for the air to go up through the vents. I have a fan on the other side blowing air out. If you remove the air canister and move the evaporator there is enough room for the intercooler there with a fan to push air. If I can get the fan to work correctly I may forgo the scoop. I am wanting to keep the car as stock looking as possible. I will let you know and post some picks when I get it done. It may be the first of next year due to the Holidays. ||||reg||1
